division-by-zero condition is not caught by a DIVISION-BY-ZERO / ARITHMETIC-ERROR handler.

SYMPTOM
  (handler-case (/ 10 0) (division-by-zero () :zero))   => signals, NOT :zero
  (handler-case (/ 10 0) (arithmetic-error () :ae))     => signals, NOT :ae
  (ignore-errors (handler-case (/ 10 0) (division-by-zero () :zero))) => NIL (fell through)
The signalled error prints "ARITHMETIC ERROR: Division by zero" but is NOT of a class that
a DIVISION-BY-ZERO (or its parent ARITHMETIC-ERROR) HANDLER-CASE clause matches.  A bare
(error () ...) clause DOES catch it, so it is an ERROR but sits outside the
ARITHMETIC-ERROR / DIVISION-BY-ZERO subtree of the condition hierarchy.

REPRO (interpreter — nothing to do with the byte-code VM; the tree-walker shows it too):
  echo '(print (handler-case (/ 10 0) (division-by-zero () :zero)))' | bocl -norc -b -quit
  => an unhandled ARITHMETIC ERROR instead of :ZERO

LIKELY CAUSE
  The kernel arithmetic code (sources/arithmetic.c / integer.c) signals division by zero via
  a generic ERROR / SIMPLE-ERROR (or an ARITHMETIC-ERROR condition object whose CLASS is not
  actually DIVISION-BY-ZERO / not on the ARITHMETIC-ERROR CPL), so TYPEP against
  DIVISION-BY-ZERO fails in the handler match.  Fix: signal a real DIVISION-BY-ZERO
  condition (subclass of ARITHMETIC-ERROR, with OPERATION / OPERANDS slots per CLHS) from the
  integer/ratio/float division paths.

FOUND-WHILE
  Writing tests/test_misc.c::test_vm_selfhost for vm-9 (a self-hosting corpus).  The corpus
  used (handler-case (/ 10 x) (division-by-zero () :zero)); it was rewritten to signal an
  explicit ERROR instead, since this pre-existing gap is orthogonal to the VM work.
